 sum(gas) < sum(cost):\n            return -1\n        start = 0\n        tank = 0\n        for i in range(len(gas)):\n            tank += gas[i] - cost[i]\n            if tank < 0:\n                start = i + 1  # no station in [start, i] can be the answer\n                tank = 0\n        return start\n","approach":"**Two facts make a single pass sufficient.** (1) If total gas >= total cost, some start works; otherwise none does. (2) If you start at `s` and the tank first goes negative after station `i`, then no station in `s..i` can be a valid start: starting later in that range only means arriving at `i` with less fuel (you skipped the non-negative surplus accumulated before). So the candidate start jumps to `i + 1`.\n\nRun the simulation once with `start = 0`; each time the tank dips below zero, reset it and move `start` past the failure point. Because of fact (1) the final `start` is valid: the segment from `start` to the end has non-negative running sum, and the wrap-around is covered because the total surplus is non-negative and all earlier segments had negative sums that the surplus exactly compensates.\n\n**Equivalent view:** with `diff[i] = gas[i] - cost[i]`, the answer is the index right after the minimum prefix sum; starting there makes every prefix non-negative.\n\n**Common mistakes:** trying every start (O(n^2), the large test makes that visibly slow), simulating two laps unnecessarily, and forgetting the single-station case.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Prove fact (2) formally. If several starts were valid, how would you return all of them? (Prefix sums: every index following a global-minimum prefix.) Driving counter-clockwise as well? (Run the same algorithm on the reversed arrays with costs shifted.) Tank capacity limit? (Then greedy breaks; it becomes a simulation per start or a sliding window problem.)","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)"},"similar":["jump-game","maximum-subarray","minimum-number-of-refueling-stops"],"number":146,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def canCompleteCircuit(self, gas: List[int], cost: List[int]) -> int:\n        pass\n"}